[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152196 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c08a79-bba6-544e-a9ea-ca58b06c0451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407856} {:answer/id #uuid "8adf8b3d-ac7a-52cf-8b4c-03f7c2608d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407869} {:answer/id #uuid "9d38aa51-050c-524f-9c39-3aa01c15091e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407874} {:answer/id #uuid "dae716d0-1847-54c9-9892-a356fbf81aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4388827-d9f0-5814-8367-e64cdc9001a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8adf8b3d-ac7a-52cf-8b4c-03f7c2608d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407869} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bb5409-539d-5019-8102-b7a8fb7859d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c274592-717b-5063-8a47-5f35487eca60" :question/math-academy-id "q-80091" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9df0c9dd8b76ac337b6110fc92019924ad2ee1c4cc0da8216c27709b641a8451.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(0)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(0)$ but $-2\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c351e5d5-1ce7-5f17-b3aa-950de378bb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151446} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0a9dd5-6274-524a-87d3-f4cfb805a450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151447} {:answer/id #uuid "06423f7d-572d-5cad-9b6e-592ddb7e000c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151448} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec68817-7d8f-59cc-9760-6c6003093474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151449} {:answer/id #uuid "64e44bf7-a536-5c69-91a0-9ae9bb3d20d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151450}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bc235602-ec15-54ae-b3a9-269766a92938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151445} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b71f69-e1d8-53c9-9f61-71b69052ec3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382339} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec5d919-368f-557b-9f85-72244b9a968d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382340} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cfbf88-977c-56df-889f-77cdd4106738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382341} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ffb88b-f014-584f-9b8a-729178b1a50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382342} {:answer/id #uuid "86389fe4-b95b-58b4-9365-60c3903f43fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b71f69-e1d8-53c9-9f61-71b69052ec3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382339} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa03635-4c62-5bed-9ed3-91b36181c7a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75838641-c52f-5634-aa18-714596e405a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80265" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee5e9b6-3e97-56e9-853f-9d72f1154481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345622} {:answer/id #uuid "23faa8ae-d9f3-5f57-b21e-6362df73adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345682} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfde730-bed7-5b3c-a445-7ce2d3fb6c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345866} {:answer/id #uuid "47f8e205-cafd-57bb-b6b4-2dd8d3754108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} {:answer/id #uuid "dcee67be-ab48-5537-8451-b5d9a776340f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47f8e205-cafd-57bb-b6b4-2dd8d3754108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345968} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a852fd7-7eb9-5d40-a916-7e4688b70024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e648a84-4fc0-5e89-9abf-3bfad9d2cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-80266" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/73/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec8a402-d0f9-5343-90f0-1b6233ea64b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345765} {:answer/id #uuid "3377d9e6-7f7a-5fbe-8eff-29979aea8010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345798} {:answer/id #uuid "3e68ec9d-647e-5f3d-b6ea-f9f460b6dd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345879} {:answer/id #uuid "d634b729-708b-5c4e-9295-72f200c0b153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347237} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf64718-29fc-50fb-8b11-43d12dc57bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3377d9e6-7f7a-5fbe-8eff-29979aea8010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2c5203-4283-560e-911e-00b61a6a5a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f66ee2a6-cdd7-5b19-895e-1ea6e9717b7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80267" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-6$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/76/760595b548b2327b9c7c44f336d2c0c2b6a69688c887587f162f8ff2ac5bb5ab.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160b1926-1bcd-5c5b-bd7d-bf6e5649f985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345543} {:answer/id #uuid "305027c1-06e6-56de-a556-2b5230d47d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345779} {:answer/id #uuid "804f49a5-d816-55a4-a4ff-34a63af08cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346473} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f9c0f2-e674-513c-82fb-0d2f5a40569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346789} {:answer/id #uuid "f082cd67-0546-5f90-8651-029089a3b6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "305027c1-06e6-56de-a556-2b5230d47d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0749705b-ee1b-5d79-b079-3b1d50ac9f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca2c9d4-83c1-5596-b062-1537df902124" :question/math-academy-id "q-80425" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d09a23a1ae9f737c7f19ba83d6d20c3fbcea3c7deb71fa307264c90afabc6580.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/16/1620acdcfd3783df478322ebe5e81cb274d908db12d9c8bdc701fe21070b828f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23853c33-d0ed-571b-9440-f3644fa48df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345675} {:answer/id #uuid "3e36c1ae-af25-5605-87dc-f2ece1a31876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345878} {:answer/id #uuid "84837529-249c-5b0e-9cd2-843e73c7c6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346506} {:answer/id #uuid "b61b47b3-7047-55ed-9f36-f1cf4c9519b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346937} {:answer/id #uuid "d24b6135-f0aa-5dca-ba04-518c8974508c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61b47b3-7047-55ed-9f36-f1cf4c9519b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346937} :answer-field/id #uuid "0220025d-38d4-5517-bac4-a948b572b394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8098b383-f4a9-5d0e-9352-b359bad47678" :question/math-academy-id "q-80430" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7db51d6af763e8853f815feb6e74b88d0ca9a19e79c42b3a42da4e8b088f93c.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20028793-1c72-5a52-b4e2-b60c21937a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345634} {:answer/id #uuid "586425ce-ee0c-5057-a7c2-0f0539fded11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346109} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1f4ef8-e587-5290-a63d-c0e6b3f1ef70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346138} {:answer/id #uuid "70a79bab-463d-50e7-bc03-5e334e918639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346341} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee9293-c5ee-5269-90c1-82c0a24d51f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c1f4ef8-e587-5290-a63d-c0e6b3f1ef70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346138} :answer-field/id #uuid "927fd24c-ceef-5a37-8b0a-ee9628364f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ba1b157-d35e-5ebb-b99d-e367dc0feceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80438" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8392135cf2ffbfd91d51fee015cc4ddc6778b890e40e1f63eae18b6927e85e8.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{R}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22bcdfb2-485a-5242-a5b5-dd9ef8cee027" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90b73970-58db-5b9a-a9ea-d40532ba0dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151560} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8c1398-7a9a-5686-98b5-381d0a13497c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151561} {:answer/id #uuid "011c5699-bae3-5953-a5d1-bc87bb87e3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151562} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb92217-7832-5b7d-94ff-accd909fae78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151563} {:answer/id #uuid "6be70c1d-8b57-59bf-83c9-7254d13bd495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151564}] :answer-field/id #uuid "423ddc1e-34ff-57b8-a124-cf44977a4cb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85cb9fae-86b1-5b2e-9d0d-16532d0edd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385763} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b620ff-b07a-539e-848b-5a4495a02b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385764} {:answer/id #uuid "3d20a11b-a7f0-5f86-a73e-b42148ebad7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385765} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa6b498-2dd2-5cb7-8ced-5dc9ba664481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385766} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbfccba-c8ae-5eaa-971e-31abfa9ab07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b620ff-b07a-539e-848b-5a4495a02b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385764} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bf18a4-4f80-5aef-aed9-a5c5b7c38441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b7fec18-8819-50a6-ad9e-b4237d8c4fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-82109" :question/problem "![](images/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152276 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de06ca6-8f00-5b6f-912b-eb57f6ed18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151740} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa9e613-8613-5512-a847-02218cdbbfb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151741} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa970b7-b02f-5076-8f36-1ed0b084a913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151742} {:answer/id #uuid "602c09fd-f9f6-5adc-83bb-fc0bb70218a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151743} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8602ec-8060-5761-b4bb-e8d0b17ea0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151744}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cd90e6db-ef54-5dca-a2a0-2fe10b67ce91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151739} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebd4f6a-e711-5a30-817f-632a3798bec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404607} {:answer/id #uuid "fa61a26f-ad52-5dd6-9a0a-58b7b29605f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404608} {:answer/id #uuid "df920d21-8207-5b52-a72a-8fc3592e0c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404609} {:answer/id #uuid "cb49a1af-87c1-5d9f-814f-b3600e3126c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404610} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd96fc6-a365-548a-a677-09d28338ad35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df920d21-8207-5b52-a72a-8fc3592e0c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404609} :answer-field/id #uuid "a620906d-6f92-5060-b907-57c31554da32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b755a996-db20-51e4-9f08-2f14dd2e4212" :question/math-academy-id "q-93952" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0$\nII. $8{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=10$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+1,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d28a50a6-7308-5e7d-9506-1e4175511186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151776} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8b1e51-4f39-5465-8987-f4a307226cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151777} {:answer/id #uuid "171962fe-cdfc-56f5-897b-aa03946cf77e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151778} {:answer/id #uuid "61310617-8554-57e3-9ce9-7002b33a7a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151779} {:answer/id #uuid "643cedf7-507f-5e63-91cf-0aa5f8a535ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151780}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4be8de-607c-5578-91b6-081cf9216fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151775}] :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $-2$ is a natural number\n2. $-2$ is an integer\n3. $-2$ is a rational number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf2e96a-8b3a-5175-a84c-79bb426aa3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305253} {:answer/id #uuid "1e830247-ccc1-59cf-8ab1-52e97736b3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305440} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2b201d-68ed-5c22-a614-3f40b0102596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306048} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} {:answer/id #uuid "a41a73eb-f8d7-52b1-8075-5729f765c02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4d336c-e643-5ced-921e-470ef6e9ebbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a891d9-a977-5225-95b8-2aaad9af9dd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0a1373b-0f2f-546e-8300-5cb2dc8f926e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334693" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0045e19-a41d-5611-82d7-7ad5c5aeb0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155596} {:answer/id #uuid "d76f1519-15e9-5b90-a1aa-199641660bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155597} {:answer/id #uuid "edfc9967-db33-5130-89e8-53ddfb507dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155598} {:answer/id #uuid "41cb39c8-ff54-5498-8100-eb079e9a807d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155599} {:answer/id #uuid "5684e00f-5151-5265-aced-d94071a413bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155600}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ff44977-be3c-5885-8387-dbd95362e0b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac3dbdb-034d-5abb-9097-9d55a19654e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385674} {:answer/id #uuid "45439c8a-036c-5058-baee-1cd55320911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385675} {:answer/id #uuid "605597d4-9e2e-532d-bf22-794f29454241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385676} {:answer/id #uuid "27f91c21-33b8-5cf9-ae3a-9cbe6ffd3558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385677} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d74aa-ecae-5506-b7c6-246b7220ee79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605597d4-9e2e-532d-bf22-794f29454241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385676} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bcdfb0e-595a-5b87-938e-f5368b2831a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59712b43-1263-520b-8160-c638c0e80547" :question/math-academy-id "q-80085" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $n>2,$ then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $n=0$\n- $0>1$\n- ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,⇒\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{⏞}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f828cb-c8b1-5da4-9711-15ace712dffa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Vacuous Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db6844f-3b9b-547e-baee-4abbab43193f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155608} {:answer/id #uuid "b876a24e-c5f0-5357-a4dd-d97b322ff71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155609} {:answer/id #uuid "c104ab36-4ac0-522a-9eb0-3d3a83681ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155610} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1f931b-84b7-598a-9e1e-173602ef1fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155611} {:answer/id #uuid "24ca7b81-1ae7-564d-bd5a-d11d65439504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155612}] :answer-field/id #uuid "aab9cb5d-be8f-53d8-8326-74fddbf4b70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed58a446-296f-56a4-88b3-e066f2653784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385680} {:answer/id #uuid "ee33778f-5e87-5116-9860-f133bf9a69ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385681} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd9478a-ad11-5b70-9921-1b2721fdd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385682} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa31f9-05da-5475-a573-96801110c1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385683} {:answer/id #uuid "a5023795-1f7c-5581-a250-76ca3c12abca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee33778f-5e87-5116-9860-f133bf9a69ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385681} :answer-field/id #uuid "868dfbfb-36d3-5a55-a0ee-0c52dbdca758" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb09ac10-eb5c-5627-9b3a-f58793f9c5b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80089" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $n>0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $n<0$\n- $0>1$\n- ${n}^{2}<0$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f645c498-658b-5078-9fb6-4e4c5b6c865d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155686} {:answer/id #uuid "1d30b730-6613-56c6-96cb-30e7